@@ -1,7 +1,16 @@
 Exception Handling
 ##################
 
-Schedule doesn't catch exceptions that happen during job execution. Therefore any exceptions thrown during job execution will bubble up and interrupt schedule's run_xyz function.
+Schedule doesn't swallow exceptions that happen during job execution.
+
+When ``run_pending()`` is called and a job raises, the other jobs that are due
+in the same call still run. Once they have all run, the first exception that
+was raised is re-raised, so your process still sees the failure. If several
+jobs fail, only the first exception is re-raised (the others are logged).
+A failed job is not rescheduled and stays due, so it is retried on the next
+``run_pending()`` call.
+
+``run_all()`` is unchanged: an exception interrupts it immediately.
 
 If you want to guard against exceptions you can wrap your job function
 in a decorator like this:
